  "output": "## Scope note first\n\nThis is a two-layer codebase and the layers are at very different stages:\n\n- **`web/`** (Next.js console) is substantially built and, honestly, already disciplined about the failure modes you asked about — batched queries, `Promise.all` for independent reads, one Client Component in the whole review path. Findings below are real but modest.\n- **`engine/`** (Python pipeline, matching, detection, resolution agent) is **100% stub code** — every function in `pipeline/blocking.py`, `pipeline/detect.py`, `pipeline/normalize.py`, `agent/graph.py`, `db.py`, and `main.py`'s `execute_run` is `raise NotImplementedError(...)` (Phase 1–3 per `AGENTS.md`'s build order haven't landed). There are no N+1 patterns, leaks, or redundant computations to find there yet — I flag the two places the design docs themselves call out as the highest-risk spots for when that code gets written, instead of inventing findings against code that doesn't exist.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Missing indexes (the highest-leverage finding, cross-cutting)\n\n`web/lib/db/schema.ts` defines exactly one index (`entities_run_source_external_idx`). Every other foreign key and filter/sort column relies on a sequential scan. The queue, audit, and export queries you already read (`web/lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `runs.ts`, `audit.ts`, `export.ts`) hit these hard:\n\n| Table.column | Hit by | How |\n|---|---|---|\n| `conflicts.runId` | `getQueuePage`, `countFiltered`, `getQueueCounts`, `getSeverityBreakdown`, `loadDecisions`, `countConflicts` | `WHERE`, every one |\n| `conflicts.matchId` | `baseQuery()` | join predicate |\n| `matches.entityAId` / `entityBId` | `baseQuery()` | join predicate, twice |\n| `resolutions.conflictId` | `baseQuery()`, `getReviewTarget` | `LEFT JOIN`, on the hottest path (every PATCH review re-reads it) |\n| `auditLog.runId`, `auditLog.createdAt` | `listRunAudit`, `getAuditPage` | filter + `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` |\n| `entities.parentExternalId` (+ `runId`) | `countChildrenByParent` | `WHERE runId = ? AND parentExternalId IN (...)`, the existing composite index (`runId, sourceId, externalId`) doesn't cover this |\n| `runs.startedAt` | `listRuns`, `getLatestRunWithConflicts` | `ORDER BY ... LIMIT` |\n\nAt demo scale (hundreds of conflicts) this is invisible. At the scale the codebase's own comments describe — \"a reviewer working a queue of 400,\" a 500-account synthetic run, a 50k-account org — every one of these becomes a full-table scan on every queue page load and every review action. This is a migration, not a code change:\n\n```sql\n-- web/drizzle/0002_index_hot_paths.sql\nCREATE INDEX conflicts_run_idx ON conflicts (run_id);\nCREATE INDEX conflicts_match_idx ON conflicts (match_id);\nCREATE INDEX matches_entity_a_idx ON matches (entity_a_id);\nCREATE INDEX matches_entity_b_idx ON matches (entity_b_id);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX resolutions_conflict_idx ON resolutions (conflict_id); -- also enforces the 1:1 invariant conflicts.ts:62-66 already flags as unenforced\nCREATE INDEX audit_log_run_created_idx ON audit_log (run_id, created_at DESC);\nCREATE INDEX entities_run_parent_idx ON entities (run_id, parent_external_id);\nCREATE INDEX runs_started_at_idx ON runs (started_at DESC);\n```\n\nGenerate it properly with `npx drizzle-kit generate` after adding the `index(...)` calls to `schema.ts` (same pattern already used for `entities_run_source_external_idx`) rather than hand-writing the SQL — but that's the target shape. The `resolutions_conflict_idx` as `UNIQUE` also closes the exact gap the comment at `conflicts.ts:62-66` already calls out (\"not yet enforced by a unique index... a migration is a decision about the schema rather than about this screen\").\n\n---\n\n## 2. N+1 query patterns\n\n**None found in the implemented code.** `getQueuePage` (`conflicts.ts:172`) is the model to point at: one joined query for rows, one grouped query for counts, one grouped query for child-counts across the whole page — three round trips for however many conflicts are on the page, not one-per-row. `buildRunExport` and `getAuditPage` follow the same shape.\n\nOne real (if minor) redundant-fetch pattern, not N+1 but adjacent to it:\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.ts:24-30\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const all = await listSources();   // fetches every row incl. jsonb `config`, Zod-parses each\n  return {\n    crm: all.filter((s) => s.kind === \"salesforce\" || s.kind === \"hubspot\").length,\n    ...\n```\n\nFetching and Zod-validating every source row (config blob included) just to produce three counts is wasted work that scales with source count for no reason. Push it into SQL:\n\n```ts\nexport async function countSourcesBySide() {\n  const rows = await getDb()\n    .select({ kind: sources.kind, total: count() })\n    .from(sources)\n    .groupBy(sources.kind);\n  // fold rows into { crm, warehouse, synthetic }\n}\n```\n\n**Engine forward-look:** when `db.py`'s `write_entities`/`write_matches`/`write_conflicts` get implemented, the AGENTS.md comment on `db.py:3-5` (\"Streaming that back... adds a hop... for no benefit\") already commits to batch writes — make sure that's literally `psycopg`'s `executemany`/`copy` for the thousand-plus rows a run produces, not a loop of single-row `INSERT`s, which would be the N+1 of this codebase's write path.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\n**Also none found worth fixing.** The architecture actively prevents this class of bug: `ConflictQueue` (`conflict-queue.tsx`) is the *only* Client Component in the review path, everything else — filters, counts, rows — is server-rendered. Callbacks are `useCallback`'d correctly (`submitFor`, `move`, `setOverrideOpen`), and the `key`-based remount strategy for filter/page changes (noted in the component's own comment) is the right way to reset selection state, avoiding an effect-driven reset.\n\nThe one nitpick, not worth a ticket on its own:\n\n```ts\n// conflict-queue.tsx:137\nconst staggerIndex = useMemo(() => (index: number) => Math.min(index, 24), []);\n```\n\nThis memoizes a closure that captures nothing and does one comparison — `useMemo` buys nothing here versus just defining `const staggerIndex = (index: number) => Math.min(index, 24)` outside the component. Harmless, just unnecessary indirection.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Caching opportunities\n\n`listSources()`, `listRulesets()`, and `getActiveRuleset()` are read on nearly every console navigation (`runs/new`, `rules`, `sources`) and each forces full per-request dynamic rendering via `connection()` — correct for correctness, but this data changes rarely (`sources` via occasional CRUD, `rulesets` are append-only versions) and is re-fetched from Postgres on every request regardless.\n\nI checked `node_modules/next/dist/docs` before suggesting an API, since `web/AGENTS.md` warns this Next.js version has breaking changes from what I'd expect from training data — this project is on Next 16's **Cache Components** model, not the older `unstable_cache`. The current directive is `\"use cache\"` + `cacheLife`/`cacheTag` from `next/cache`, gated behind `cacheComponents: true` in `next.config.ts` (currently unset — this would be a config change worth calling out, not a silent one):\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.ts\nimport { cacheLife, cacheTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport async function listSources(): Promise<Source[]> {\n  \"use cache\";\n  cacheLife(\"minutes\");\n  cacheTag(\"sources\");\n\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n}\n```\n\n```ts\n// web/lib/server/sources.ts — invalidate on write\nimport { revalidateTag } from \"next/cache\";\n\nexport async function createSource(input: CreateSource): Promise<Source> {\n  const [row] = await getDb().insert(sources).values(input).returning();\n  revalidateTag(\"sources\");\n  return sourceSchema.parse(row);\n}\n```\n\nSame pattern for `rulesets` (tag `\"rulesets\"`, invalidated in `createRulesetVersion`). This is a net-new adoption, not a fix — flagging it as an opportunity per your instructions to name a library/pattern change and ask before installing anything; this doesn't add a dependency, just a config flag, but it does change the rendering model for these routes, so I'd want your sign-off before flipping `cacheComponents: true` given how new that flag is.\n\n**Engine forward-look:** `AGENTS.md` already mandates the single biggest cost lever for the agent — prompt caching on the ruleset block in every Claude call — but `agent/prompts.py` is unimplemented, so there's nothing to verify yet. Flag it for review the moment `prompts.py` lands.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Memory leaks\n\n**None found.** Both places that hold browser-side resources clean up correctly:\n\n- `RunLive`'s `Elapsed` (`run-live.tsx:98-118`): `setInterval` is cleared in the `useEffect` cleanup, and the effect re-runs only on `running`/`startedAt` changes.\n- `ConflictQueue`'s keyboard handler (`conflict-queue.tsx:130-131`): `window.addEventListener`/`removeEventListener` pair inside `useEffect`, correctly scoped.\n- `QueryProvider` (`query-provider.tsx:15-29`): `QueryClient` is created once via `useState(() => new QueryClient(...))`, not recreated per render, and is scoped to the one screen that polls (`RunLive`) rather than mounted at the root — so it's garbage-collected on navigation away, not held for the app's lifetime.\n\n**Engine forward-look — this is the one to actually watch for:** `pipeline/blocking.py` is where the embedding model (`sentence-transformers`) and FAISS index get built. The classic Python-service leak pattern is re-loading the transformer model or rebuilding the FAISS index inside the per-request/per-run function instead of once at module or process scope. Since `block()` at `blocking.py:23` is currently an empty stub, this is worth stating explicitly before it's written: load the model once (module-level or a lazily-initialized singleton), build the FAISS index once per run's candidate set, and never inside a loop over entities.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Redundant computations\n\n```ts\n// web/components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:166-179\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);              // O(n) lookup...\n  return (\n    <RuleRow\n      position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}         // ...twice, per row\n```\n\nBoth `indexOf` calls are O(n), called inside a map over n rules → O(n²) per render, and it re-runs on every keystroke in the editor (each `update()` call replaces the whole `rules` array). Rulesets are small today, so this isn't causing visible lag, but it's an easy fix and the pattern is worth not propagating:\n\n```ts\nconst orderedWithIndex = orderRulesByEvaluation(\n  rules.map((rule, index) => ({ rule, index })),\n);\n// carry `index` through instead of re-deriving it with indexOf\n```\n\n(`orderRulesByEvaluation` in `types/rules.ts:88-93` would need a version that sorts `{rule, index}` pairs rather than bare rules — small change, keeps the position numbering correct.)\n\n**Engine forward-look — the one that matters most in this whole system:** `pipeline/blocking.py`'s own docstring states the constraint the entire architecture depends on: *\"Blocking exists so the pipeline is O(n·k) not O(n²). Never compare all pairs — that is the difference between a 500-account demo and a 50,000-account org.\"* That function is currently `raise NotImplementedError`. This isn't a bug today, but it's the single highest-consequence redundant-computation risk on the roadmap, and it's explicitly named as such in the project's own docs — worth treating as a hold-the-line item in review when Phase 2 lands, since an accidental all-pairs comparison here would be silent until the eval harness runs on a dataset larger than the smoke-test size.\n",
